  <h2>Why your call is worth money</h2>
  <p>A single water damage job often runs into thousands of dollars, is usually paid by insurance, and frequently leads to a second job: the rebuild. Very few homeowners shop around at the moment they need help. Put those together and the phone call itself becomes valuable, so an entire market has grown up around getting you to call one number rather than another.</p>
  <p>That market isn't secret, but it's rarely explained to the person making the call. Here's how it works.</p>

  <h2>Six ways a call reaches a restoration company</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Who's on the other end</th><th>How money changes hands</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>The company's own number</strong></td><td>The local business that will do the work</td><td>The company pays for its own ads or listing</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>A national franchise brand</strong></td><td>Often a central call center, which dispatches the local franchise owner for your area</td><td>The franchisee pays royalties and marketing fees to the brand</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Your insurer's program</strong></td><td>A company in the insurer's or its administrator's network</td><td>The company agrees to the program's pricing and rules. See <a href="/guides/insurance-preferred-vendor-or-own-contractor/">preferred vendors</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>A personal referral</strong></td><td>Whoever your plumber, property manager, agent or HOA recommends</td><td>Referral fees per job are common</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>A call network or lead service</strong></td><td>A participating company in your area, reached through a routed number</td><td>The company or network pays per qualifying call or lead. This site works this way.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ad platforms</strong></td><td>Advertisers on search ads and Google's Local Services Ads</td><td>Pay per click or per lead. Local Services Ads involve license and background checks by Google.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The plumber's recommendation</h2>
  <p>The plumber who fixes your burst pipe is often the first professional to see the water damage, so their recommendation carries weight. In this industry, restoration companies commonly pay plumbers, and sometimes property managers and others, a fee for each job they refer.</p>
  <p>That's generally legal, and the plumber may genuinely like the company. Just know that the recommendation isn't neutral. It's fair to ask, "Do you get paid if I use them?" A good plumber won't mind the question. Either way, check the company yourself before signing.</p>
  <p>The plumber's other common line, "you need a restoration company," is usually honest. Water that has soaked into drywall, under flooring or into a ceiling rarely dries properly with household fans. If you're unsure, see <a href="/guides/small-leak-dry-it-yourself-or-call-a-pro/">dry it yourself or call a pro</a>.</p>

  <h2>Franchise brands and call centers</h2>
  <p>Several national restoration brands are franchise systems. The name on the truck is national, while the business that does your job is a locally owned franchise. Calls to the national number are often answered by a central call center and dispatched to whoever owns your territory.</p>
  <p>That can work well: shared training, standard paperwork, after-hours coverage. It also means quality varies with the local owner, not the brand. Judge the franchise that serves your address, not the commercial.</p>

  <h2>Call networks and lead services, including us</h2>
  <p>Some websites and ads don't belong to any restoration company. They answer or route your call and connect you with a participating company that serves your area, and they're paid when that connection happens. Restoration calls are among the more expensive home-service leads, which is why so many of these sites exist.</p>
  <p><strong>{SITE.brand} is one of them.</strong> Our phone line connects callers with participating local restoration companies, and those companies, or the call networks that work with them, pay us for the referral. You aren't charged for it and you don't have to hire the company you speak with. The full details are on <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works</a>.</p>
  <p>We tell you this because the same advice applies to us as to anyone: a referral is a starting point, not a verdict. Check the company before you sign.</p>

  <h2>Map listings that aren't real businesses</h2>
  <p>Restoration is one of the categories where map results attract fake listings. Common signs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keyword names,</strong> like "24/7 Water Damage Restoration [City Name]," instead of a real business name. Google's own guidelines don't allow that.</li>
    <li><strong>An address that's a mailbox store, a shared office or a house</strong> with no sign of a business, or no address at all in a category where crews need a shop.</li>
    <li><strong>Many near-identical listings</strong> across neighboring towns, all forwarding to one phone line.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews that are short, generic and posted in bursts.</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some of these listings forward calls to a real local company, so the work may be fine. The problem is that you don't know who you've hired until someone arrives.</p>

  <h2>When the company that shows up isn't the one you called</h2>
  <p>A few businesses take jobs and pass them to subcontractors, keeping a cut. Sometimes that's ordinary overflow during a storm. Sometimes it's the business model. Either way, your contract, your warranty and your dispute rights sit with whoever's name is on the paperwork you sign, which may not be the crew in your basement.</p>
  <p>Before signing, ask the crew: "Who do you work for, and whose name is on this contract?" If those answers differ, ask who is responsible if something goes wrong.</p>

  <h2>A two-minute phone check</h2>
  <p>You can ask these while the water is still running. A legitimate company answers them without hesitation.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>"What's your company's legal name, and where are you based?"</strong> Write it down. You'll check it in a minute.</li>
    <li><strong>"Will your own crew come, or a subcontractor?"</strong></li>
    <li><strong>"Are your technicians IICRC certified for water damage?"</strong> Certified firms can be checked on the IICRC locator.</li>
    <li><strong>"What does the first visit cost if I decide not to hire you?"</strong> Many companies inspect at no charge; some bill an emergency call fee. Know before they arrive.</li>
    <li><strong>"What will you ask me to sign?"</strong> A work authorization is normal. An assignment of benefits is a bigger step. See <a href="/guides/restoration-work-authorization-assignment-of-benefits/">before you sign</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Three records to check</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your state's business registry</strong>, usually run by the Secretary of State. The legal name should exist and be active.</li>
    <li><strong>Any contractor license or registration</strong> your state, county or city requires. Rules vary a lot. Mitigation alone often doesn't need a license, but rebuilding usually does.</li>
    <li><strong>The IICRC global locator,</strong> which lists certified firms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Proof of liability insurance is worth asking for too, ideally as a certificate sent to you by the insurance agent rather than a photo of one.</p>

  <h2>Warning signs, whoever referred them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Won't give a legal business name, or the name on the paperwork differs from the one on the phone.</li>
    <li>Pushes you to sign an assignment of benefits before anyone has looked at the damage.</li>
    <li>Says your insurance has "already approved" the work before you've opened a claim.</li>
    <li>Offers to cover or waive your deductible. See <a href="/guides/storm-chasers-deductible-offers-public-adjusters/">deductible offers</a>.</li>
    <li>Wants a large payment up front for emergency drying, which is normally billed after the work or to your insurer.</li>
  </ul>

